{"step_index":18,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T11:11:45Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nRULE 1 Remove these type of lines you have inside your text for each of every pages, because I have copied them  ----- Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 1 of 17                                                        RULE 2 There are flowcharts in which arrows are present and signs are present of plus minus. You don't have to remove them. You can just give in continuous format as it is. Don't remove them  Here is the text. ------------------           1. OSTEOMA DEFINITION Osteoma is a benign, slow-growing tumour composed of mature compact or cancellous bone. It commonly occurs in the skull and facial bones and rarely causes malignant transformation. CLASSIFICATION According to site Central osteoma: Develops within bone. Peripheral osteoma: Develops from the surface of bone. According to structure Compact osteoma Cancellous osteoma ETIOLOGY The exact cause is uncertain. Possible factors: Developmental abnormality Genetic factors Previous trauma Chronic irritation Associated syndromes, particularly Gardner syndrome CLINICAL FEATURES Small osteomas may be asymptomatic. When symptomatic: Slow-growing hard swelling Painless mass Facial asymmetry Headache in some cranial lesions Sinus obstruction Visual symptoms if orbital structures are involved Cosmetic deformity • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 1 of 17INVESTIGATIONS • • • • X-ray: Dense, well-defined bony lesion CT scan: Best for defining the size, location and relationship to surrounding structures. MRI: Useful when soft-tissue or intracranial involvement is suspected. Biopsy: Usually not required for a typical small osteoma but may be performed when diagnosis is uncertain. COMPLICATIONS • • • • • • Cosmetic deformity Pressure on adjacent structures Sinus obstruction Visual impairment Rare neurological complications Recurrence after incomplete excision MEDICAL MANAGEMENT • • Asymptomatic: Obs\n<truncated 29496 bytes>\nain, Vertebral compression fracture, Loss of height, Kyphosis, Fragility fracture (Hip, Wrist, Vertebrae), Difficulty walking, Reduced independence. INVESTIGATIONS • • • DXA scan: Standard test for bone mineral density. T-score thresholds apply (≥ -1.0 Normal, -1.0 to -2.5 Osteopenia, ≤-2.5 Osteoporosis). Blood investigations: Calcium, Phosphate, ALP, Vitamin D, Renal function, Thyroid studies (to identify secondary causes). X-ray: Fractures, Vertebral compression, Kyphosis. COMPLICATIONS Vertebral compression fracture, Hip fracture, Wrist fracture, Loss of height, Kyphosis, Chronic pain, Reduced mobility, Falls, Loss of independence, Increased mortality following major hip fracture. MEDICAL MANAGEMENT 1. 2. Lifestyle management: Adequate calcium & vitamin D, Weight-bearing & resistance exercise, Balance training, Smoking cessation, Limit excessive alcohol, Fall prevention. Pharmacological treatment: Bisphosphonates, Denosumab, Selective estrogen receptor modulators, Anabolic agents (teriparatide/abaloparatide), Romosozumab. Choice depends on fracture risk, age, renal function, previous fractures, contraindications. SURGICAL MANAGEMENT Osteoporosis itself is usually managed medically. Surgery is required for complications (e.g., fracture management via internal fixation, arthroplasty, vertebral augmentation for vertebral compression, hemiarthroplasty/THA for hip fractures). PHYSIOTHERAPY MANAGEMENT Goals: Improve muscle strength, Improve balance, Prevent falls, Maintain posture, Improve functional independence, Reduce fracture risk. Exercises: Weight-bearing exercise, Resistance training, Back extensor strengthening, Balance training, Postural exercises, Gait training. Orthopaedics & Metabolic Bone Diseases — University Exam Notes Page 16 of 17Precautions: Avoid high-impact activity in patients at high fracture risk, Forceful spinal flexion, Excessive twisting, Unsupervised heavy lifting.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-13T16:41:45+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
