{"step_index":24,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T11:21:00Z","content":"--- **1 OSTEOARTHRITIS** **DEFINITION** Osteoarthritis OA is a chronic progressive degenerative disorder of synovial joints characterized by loss of articular cartilage subchondral bone changes osteophyte formation and varying degrees of synovial inflammation resulting in pain stiffness and functional limitation **CAUSES / RISK FACTORS** **Primary OA** Occurs without a single identifiable cause Risk factors • Increasing age • Female sex • Obesity • Genetic predisposition • Previous joint injury • Repetitive joint loading **Secondary OA** Occurs due to an identifiable condition • Previous fracture • Joint infection • Rheumatoid arthritis • Congenital joint abnormality • Ligament injury • Meniscal injury • Avascular necrosis • Metabolic disorders **COMMON SITES** • Knee • Hip • Spine • Hands • First MTP joint • Shoulder and other joints less commonly **PATHOPHYSIOLOGY** Mechanical/biological stress ↓ Cartilage matrix degeneration ↓ Loss of proteoglycans and collagen integrity ↓ Cartilage thinning and fissuring ↓ Subchondral sclerosis ↓ Osteophyte formation ↓ Joint space narrowing ↓ Pain + stiffness + deformity **CLINICAL FEATURES** **Symptoms** • Joint pain usually worse with activity • Pain relieved by rest initially • Morning stiffness usually less than 30 minutes • Stiffness after prolonged inactivity • Difficulty walking • Difficulty climbing stairs • Reduced functional activity **Signs** • Joint tenderness • Crepitus • Reduced ROM • Bony enlargement • Joint line tenderness • Muscle weakness • Deformity in advanced disease **Hand OA** • Heberdens nodes DIP joints • Bouchards nodes PIP joints **DEFORMITIES** **Knee** • Varus deformity • Valgus deformity **Hip** • Flexion deformity • External rotation • Adduction deformity **Hands** • Heberdens and Bouchards nodes **RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES** X ray shows • Non uniform joint space narrowing • Osteophytes • Subchondral sclerosis • Subchondral cysts • Bony remode\n<truncated 25516 bytes>\n • Genetic predisposition • Autoimmune dysregulation • Environmental triggers **CLINICAL FEATURES** **Musculoskeletal** • Polyarthritis • Arthralgia • Myalgia • Proximal muscle weakness **Vascular** • Raynaud phenomenon **Skin** • Puffy fingers • Sclerodactyly like changes • Rash **Other** • Dysphagia • Esophageal dysfunction • Interstitial lung disease • Pulmonary hypertension • Fatigue **DEFORMITIES** May develop • Joint deformity • Contractures • Sclerodactyly • Muscle wasting • Reduced hand function **COMPLICATIONS** • Pulmonary hypertension • Interstitial lung disease • Esophageal dysfunction • Cardiac involvement • Renal involvement usually less prominent than in classic SLE • Severe muscle weakness • Functional disability **RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES** No single pathognomonic radiographic appearance May demonstrate • Joint space narrowing • Soft tissue swelling • Erosive arthritis in some patients • Interstitial lung disease on chest X ray/HRCT • Muscle inflammation/edema on MRI **INVESTIGATIONS** **Blood** • ANA • Anti U1 RNP • CBC • ESR/CRP • CK • Renal and liver function **Other** • Pulmonary function tests • Echocardiography • HRCT chest where indicated • MRI muscle if myositis is suspected **CONSERVATIVE / MEDICAL MANAGEMENT** Treatment depends on the dominant manifestations May include • NSAIDs • Corticosteroids • Hydroxychloroquine • Methotrexate • Other immunosuppressive drugs • Vasodilators for Raynaud phenomenon • Treatment for pulmonary hypertension • Treatment of interstitial lung disease **Physiotherapy** • ROM exercises • Muscle strengthening • Aerobic conditioning • Breathing exercises • Hand therapy • Joint protection • Energy conservation • Functional training **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** Usually not required for the primary disease May be considered for • Severe joint destruction • Fixed deformity • Contractures • Digital complications • Joint replacement in selected cases","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm considering the best way to approach problem-solving using available tools. Specifically, I'm now focused on tool selection. My current thinking prioritizes using the most specific tool for a given task, such as a file viewer instead of a general terminal. I'm exploring how this impacts efficiency and accuracy in problem-solving.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Choices**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on avoiding suboptimal tool usage, especially `cat`, `grep`, `ls`, and `sed`, as I recall the explicit instructions. I'm focusing on only using the right tool for each job. I now understand I need to process text from a PDF, starting from page 1 and ending at page 31, but the file only has 34 pages.\n\n\n**Confirming Final Page**\n\nI've verified that page 31 is indeed the correct ending point for the text extraction. I've confirmed the stopping point is \"Joint replacement in selected cases\", and I'm now ready to start assembling the raw text by compiling the OCR data from page 1, which begins with \"1. OSTEOARTHRITIS,\" to the end of page 31. This includes several different arthritis diagnoses.\n\n\n**Analyzing OCR Artifacts**\n\nI'm now grappling with OCR artifacts. I'm focusing on how the optical character recognition (OCR) software misinterprets bullet points, placing them separately from the intended text. The data flow isn't ideal. I need to make some manual corrections. I'm working to accurately integrate the bullet points into the text, adhering to the PDF's intended format despite the OCR's quirks.\n\n\n**Reintegrating Bullet Points**\n\nI'm now carefully addressing the OCR's detachment of bullet points from their corresponding text. I've pinpointed their correct positions: before each item within \"Risk factors,\" \"Secondary OA,\" and \"COMMON SITES.\" The counts align perfectly, allowing accurate bullet insertion. I will ensure these are inserted precisely where they belong, as instructed, maintaining the PDF's origi