well as the medical and physiotherapy options. I'm noting specific interventions for pulmonary and other issues, as well as surgical considerations for severe cases, ending the review of this document.\n\n\n","truncated_fields":["content","thinking"]}
{"step_index":25,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T11:25:10Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nRule number one is    Remove page numbers written like page 1 Page 2, and so on                                                         And here is the text. -------. CERVICOBRACHIAL SYNDROME Definition Cervicobrachial syndrome is a clinical condition characterized by pain, paraesthesia, numbness, weakness or other neurological symptoms originating from the cervical spine or surrounding structures and radiating into the shoulder, arm, forearm or hand. Causes / Etiology • Cervical intervertebral disc prolapse • Cervical spondylosis and osteophytes • Foraminal or spinal canal stenosis • Whiplash and other cervical trauma • Muscle spasm and postural dysfunction • Brachial plexus lesions • Tumour, infection or inflammatory disorders • Thoracic outlet disorders Pathophysiology Cervical pathology → nerve-root irritation/compression → inflammation and impaired nerve conduction → pain and paraesthesia → weakness and functional limitation. Clinical Features • Neck pain with radiation to shoulder/upper limb • Burning, shooting or electric-shock-like pain • Paraesthesia and numbness in a dermatomal pattern • Muscle weakness and reduced grip strength • Reduced deep tendon reflexes corresponding to the affected root • Restricted cervical ROM and muscle spasm • Symptoms may worsen with cervical movement or compression Investigations • Cervical X-ray for degenerative or bony changes • MRI for disc pathology, nerve-root or cord compression • CT for bony/foraminal abnormalities • EMG/NCV when nerve involvement needs clarification • Neurological examination of dermatomes, myotomes and reflexes Complications • Chronic pain Page 1• Persistent sensory deficit • Motor weakness and muscle wasting • Loss of hand function • Reduced work capacity • Cervical myelopathy in severe cord compression Conservative / Medical Management • Relative rest and activity modification • Analgesics/NSAIDs as indicated • Muscle relaxants in selected cases • Neuropathic pain \n<truncated 13310 bytes>\nth or without radiating symptoms. Clinical Features • Deep buttock pain • Pain radiating into posterior thigh • Sciatic-type symptoms • Paraesthesia or numbness • Pain aggravated by prolonged sitting • Tenderness over piriformis • Pain with resisted hip external rotation • Pain with piriformis stretching • Possible reduced hip ROM Special Tests • FAIR test • Freiberg sign • Pace sign • Beatty manoeuvre Differential Diagnosis • Lumbar radiculopathy • Lumbar disc prolapse • Sacroiliac joint dysfunction • Hip pathology • Hamstring pathology • Spinal stenosis Investigations Page 13• Primarily a clinical diagnosis • MRI lumbar spine or pelvis to exclude other pathology • EMG/NCV in selected cases • Ultrasound in selected cases • Diagnostic local injection in selected persistent cases Complications • Chronic buttock pain • Persistent sciatic symptoms • Reduced walking and sitting tolerance • Sleep disturbance • Reduced athletic performance • Functional disability Conservative / Physiotherapy Management • Activity modification and avoidance of prolonged sitting • Heat/cold or TENS when appropriate • Piriformis, hip-rotator and gluteal stretching • Gluteus maximus and medius strengthening • Core strengthening • Neural mobilization when appropriate • Gait/running biomechanics correction • Gradual return to sport • Analgesics/NSAIDs when appropriate • Selected image-guided injection for persistent symptoms Surgical Management • Rarely required • Considered after appropriate conservative treatment in severe persistent cases with convincing evidence of sciatic nerve compression • Piriformis release • Sciatic nerve decompression • Correction of associated structural abnormalities Conclusion A careful differential diagnosis is essential because lumbar radiculopathy and other hip/spinal disorders can mimic piriformis syndrome.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-13T16:55:10+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
