{"step_index":27,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T11:33:16Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n1. CEREBRAL PALSY Definition Cerebral palsy is a group of permanent disorders of movement and posture causing activity limitation, attributed to non-progressive disturbances occurring in the developing fetal or infant brain. It may be accompanied by disturbances of sensation, perception, cognition, communication, behaviour and epilepsy. Causes / risk factors • Prenatal: congenital brain malformations, intrauterine infections, genetic abnormalities, maternal diabetes or hypertension and placental abnormalities • Perinatal: prematurity, low birth weight, hypoxic-ischaemic injury, intracranial haemorrhage and neonatal seizures • Postnatal: meningitis, encephalitis, head injury, cerebrovascular injury, severe hypoxia, kernicterus and hypoglycaemia Classification • Spastic CP: hemiplegia, diplegia and quadriplegia • Dyskinetic CP: dystonia and choreoathetosis • Ataxic CP: poor balance, incoordination and tremor • Mixed CP: combination of motor patterns Clinical features • Delayed motor milestones • Abnormal muscle tone: spasticity, dystonia or hypotonia • Weakness and poor selective motor control • Abnormal reflexes and persistence of primitive reflexes • Abnormal gait and poor balance • Contractures and deformities • Hip displacement, scoliosis and foot deformities • Seizures • Visual/hearing impairment • Speech, communication, feeding or swallowing difficulties • Cognitive or behavioural difficulties in some individuals Complications • Contractures • Joint deformities • Hip subluxation/dislocation • Scoliosis • Osteopenia • Pain • Reduced mobility • Epilepsy • Feeding and respiratory problems • Reduced independence and participation Investigations• Neurological and developmental assessment • MRI brain • EEG when seizures are suspected • Hip radiographs when indicated • Spine radiographs when indicated • Vision and hearing assessment • Functional assessment Medical management • Individualized multidisciplinary management • Spasti\n<truncated 10609 bytes>\nand current national/WHO guidance • Early treatment helps prevent nerve damage and disability Management of reactions • Type 1 reaction may cause inflammation and acute nerve impairment • Type 2 reaction may cause fever, painful nodules and systemic inflammation • Corticosteroids and other specialist-directed treatment may be required depending on the reaction Surgical management • Selected nerve decompression • Tendon transfer for claw hand • Tendon transfer for foot drop • Contracture correction • Reconstructive procedures • Ulcer management • Eyelid procedures for lagophthalmos Physiotherapy management • Assess sensation, muscle power, ROM, deformity, skin integrity, balance and gait • Daily skin inspection and protective footwear • Avoid extreme heat and barefoot walking • ROM, stretching and strengthening of functioning muscles • Balance and functional training• Claw hand: splinting, ROM, strengthening and post-tendon-transfer rehabilitation • Foot drop: AFO, gait training and balance training • Ulcer prevention: pressure relief, off-loading, appropriate footwear and education Multidisciplinary approach • Dermatologist/physician • Neurologist • Physiotherapist • Occupational therapist • Orthopaedic/plastic surgeon • Ophthalmologist • Foot-care services where available • Psychologist and social worker Prevention • Early diagnosis • Prompt MDT treatment • Contact examination/surveillance • Appropriate preventive strategies for eligible contacts • Health education • Early recognition of nerve involvement • Disability prevention and self-care education Conclusion Leprosy is a chronic infectious disease with important neurological and disability-related consequences. Early diagnosis and MDT prevent progression, while physiotherapy, orthoses, reconstructive surgery and self-care education help prevent disability and maintain function.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-13T17:03:16+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
