{"step_index":5,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:43:10Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nSPECIAL TESTS — LUMBO-SACRAL REGION I. TEST FOR LUMBAR INSTABILITY 1. PASSIVE LUMBAR EXTENSION TEST PURPOSE To assess lumbar spine instability. PROCEDURE • • • • Patient lies prone. Examiner lifts both the patient’s legs together by holding around the ankles. The legs are elevated while the knees remain extended. Observe the patient’s symptoms and lumbar response. POSITIVE SIGN • • Development/reproduction of low-back pain Feeling of heaviness or instability in the lumbar region. INDICATES Lumbar segmental instability. II. TESTS FOR MUSCLE DYSFUNCTION 2. 90–90 STRAIGHT LEG RAISING TEST PURPOSE To assess hamstring muscle tightness/flexibility. PROCEDURE • • • • Patient lies supine. Flex the hip and knee to approximately 90°. Stabilize the thigh. Slowly extend the knee while maintaining hip flexion. POSITIVE SIGN • • Inability to achieve full knee extension. Increased resistance/tightness in the posterior thigh. INDICATES Hamstring tightness/shortness. 3. TRENDELENBURG TEST PURPOSE To assess hip abductor muscle function, especially gluteus medius. PROCEDURE • • • Patient stands on one leg. Ask the patient to lift the opposite foot from the floor. Observe the position of the pelvis. POSITIVE SIGN • Pelvis drops on the side of the lifted/non-weight-bearing leg. INDICATES Weakness of the hip abductors of the weight-bearing leg, particularly gluteus medius.III. TESTS FOR NEUROLOGICAL DYSFUNCTION 4. STRAIGHT LEG RAISING TEST (SLR/LASÈGUE’S TEST) PURPOSE To assess lumbosacral nerve-root irritation, particularly due to PIVD. PROCEDURE • • • • Patient lies supine. Keep the knee fully extended. Slowly raise the patient’s straight leg by flexing the hip. Note the angle and symptoms produced. POSITIVE SIGN • Reproduction of radicular/sciatic pain, typically between about 30–70° of hip flexion. INDICATES Lumbosacral nerve-root irritation/compression, commonly associated with PIVD. 5. SLUMP TEST PURPOSE To assess neural tissue mechanosensitivity, particul\n<truncated 1200 bytes>\ne. Place the tested leg in: Hip flexion Abduction External rotation ◦ Place the lateral ankle over the opposite thigh. Stabilize the opposite pelvis. Gently press the flexed knee downward. POSITIVE SIGN • • • Pain reproduced in the hip/groin → suggests hip pathology. Pain in the sacroiliac region → may indicate SI joint pathology. Restricted ROM may also be significant. INDICATES Hip or sacroiliac joint dysfunction.8. GAENSLEN’S TEST PURPOSE To assess sacroiliac joint dysfunction/pathology. PROCEDURE • • • • Patient lies near the edge of the table. One hip and knee are maximally flexed toward the chest. The opposite leg is allowed to hang off the edge of the table. Apply gentle pressure to produce opposite movements at the pelvis. POSITIVE SIGN • Reproduction of pain in the sacroiliac region. INDICATES Sacroiliac joint dysfunction. 9. GILLET’S TEST PURPOSE To assess sacroiliac joint mobility. PROCEDURE • • • • Patient stands. Examiner palpates the PSIS and sacrum. Patient flexes one hip and knee, lifting the foot from the floor. Observe movement of the PSIS relative to the sacrum. POSITIVE SIGN • Abnormal or restricted movement of the PSIS compared with the opposite side. INDICATES Sacroiliac joint hypomobility/dysfunction. 10. MARCH’S TEST PURPOSE To assess sacroiliac joint movement/dysfunction. PROCEDURE • • • • Patient stands. Examiner palpates the PSIS. Patient marches by lifting one knee at a time. Observe the movement of the PSIS. POSITIVE SIGN • Abnormal/asymmetrical movement of the PSIS. INDICATES Sacroiliac joint dysfunction or altered mobility.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-14T15:13:10+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\n<USER_SETTINGS_CHANGE>\nThe user changed setting `Model Selection` from Gemini 3.1 Pro (High) to Gemini 3.1 Pro (Low). No need to comment on this change if the