{"step_index":16,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:57:17Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-14T09:57:04Z sender=9e162f78-e95f-47da-b0ba-af19f50adfd4 priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=---**I INSTABILITY TESTS**---**1 APPREHENSION TEST** Purpose To assess anterior shoulder instability Procedure Abduct the shoulder to 90 flex the elbow to 90 and slowly externally rotate the shoulder Positive Sign Patient becomes apprehensive or fears shoulder dislocation Indicates Anterior glenohumeral instability---**2 JERK TEST** Purpose To assess posterior shoulder instability and posterior labral lesions Procedure Shoulder abducted to 90 and internally rotated Apply axial compression through the elbow while horizontally adducting the arm Positive Sign Painful clunk or jerk Indicates Posterior instability or posterior labral tear---**3 DRAWER TEST** Purpose To assess anterior and posterior glenohumeral instability Procedure Stabilize the scapula and translate the humeral head anteriorly and posteriorly Positive Sign Excessive humeral head movement compared to the opposite side Indicates Shoulder instability---**4 SULCUS SIGN** Purpose To assess inferior glenohumeral instability Procedure Apply downward traction to the relaxed arm Positive Sign Visible sulcus below the acromion Indicates Inferior or multidirectional shoulder instability---**II IMPINGEMENT TESTS**---**1 HAWKINS KENNEDY TEST** Purpose Assess subacromial impingement Procedure Flex shoulder and elbow to 90 and internally rotate the shoulder Positive Sign Pain Indicates Subacromial impingement---**2 NEERS IMPINGEMENT TEST** Purpose Assess subacromial impingement Procedure Stabilize the scapula and passively flex the internally rotated arm Positive Sign Pain Indicates Subacromial impingement---**3 PAINFUL ARC TEST** Purpose Assess shoulder impingement Procedure Ask the patient to actively abduct the shoulder Positive Sign Pain between 60 120 of abduction Indicates Subacr\n<truncated 1287 bytes>\nhoulder flexion and abduction with light weights while the examiner observes scapular motion Positive Sign Scapular winging or asymmetrical movement Indicates Scapular dyskinesia---**2 SCAPULAR LOAD TEST SCAPULAR ASSISTANCE TEST** Purpose Assess the effect of scapular assistance on shoulder movement Procedure Examiner manually assists upward rotation and posterior tilt of the scapula during arm elevation Positive Sign Pain decreases or shoulder movement improves Indicates Scapular dyskinesia---**V MUSCLE PATHOLOGY TESTS**---**1 YERGASONS TEST** Purpose Assess biceps tendon pathology Procedure Patient supinates the forearm against resistance with the elbow flexed to 90 Positive Sign Pain or snapping over the bicipital groove Indicates Biceps tendinitis or transverse humeral ligament injury---**2 SPEEDS TEST** Purpose Assess long head of biceps tendon Procedure Shoulder flexed to 90 elbow extended forearm supinated resist downward pressure Positive Sign Pain in the bicipital groove Indicates Biceps tendinitis or SLAP lesion---**3 LIFT OFF TEST** Purpose Assess Subscapularis muscle Procedure Place the dorsum of the hand on the lower back and ask the patient to lift the hand away Positive Sign Unable to lift the hand or marked weakness Indicates Subscapularis tear or weakness---**4 TEST FOR SUPRASPINATUS** Purpose Assess supraspinatus muscle Procedure Perform the Empty Can Test against resistance Positive Sign Pain or weakness Indicates Supraspinatus pathology---**5 TEST FOR INFRASPINATUS** Purpose Assess infraspinatus muscle Procedure Patient externally rotates the shoulder against resistance with the elbow flexed to 90 Positive Sign Pain or weakness Indicates Infraspinatus tear or weakness---